;\n}\n```\n\nSame logic as C. Good.\n\nBut wait... `blake256_update` operates on bits-counted input. So `(inlen + 4) as u64 * 8` would be the bits. In my mgf1 I have:\n```rust\nblake256(&mut out[out_pos..out_pos + SPX_BLAKE256_OUTPUT_BYTES], &inbuf, (inlen + 4) as u64);\n```\n\nThe C code does:\n```c\nblake256(out, inbuf, inlen + 4);\n```\n\nWhere `blake256` does `update(.., inlen*8)`. OK so input len in BYTES, not bits. My Rust blake256 does `*8` internally. Good.\n\nLet me check `hash_message` for blake. But first let me check if `prf_addr` matches. Actually let me write a simple comparison test."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":626,"cache_read_input_tokens":350659,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":626,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0973e908-1840-49ab-9a3a-eab056f878a1","uuid":"c34714cc-fad0-42f7-ad08-0710f022ac68"}
